        ("h", "5.4 Functions"),
        ("p", "A function is a named block of work. **Predefined** functions come with the language or its library, such as `sqrt` in `cmath`. **User-defined** functions are the ones you write. Functions let you name a task, test it once, and call it from several places. A large program becomes a set of small ones."),
        ("p", "The **signature** is the function's name, its parameter list, and its return type. A **prototype** declares the function before main. The **definition** contains the body. A **call** uses the function. Formal parameters are the names in the definition. Actual parameters are the values sent at the call."),
        ("p", "A **local variable** exists only inside the block where it is declared. A **global variable** is declared outside functions and can be used by all of them; use it rarely, because any function can change it. A **static local variable** keeps its value between calls to that function."),
        ("p", "An **inline** function is a short function that the compiler may place at the point of the call, avoiding a call for a one-line calculation. `exit`, from `cstdlib`, ends the whole program at once. A `return` statement leaves only the current function and can carry a value back to the caller."),
