        {"q": "What is cascading of the output stream? Write one statement.", "a": "Cascading means using << more than once in a single statement. Example: cout << \"Sum = \" << a + b << endl; The text and the number are sent out by the same statement."},
        {"q": "Differentiate while and do-while.", "a": "while tests the condition before the body, so the body may not run at all. do-while runs the body and then tests, so the body runs at least once. do-while suits a menu that must appear before the user chooses."},
        {"q": "Differentiate formal and actual parameters.", "a": "Formal parameters are the names in the function heading, such as int a and int b in int larger(int a, int b). Actual parameters are the values at the call, such as larger(x, y). The actual values are passed into the formal parameters."},
        {"q": "Why can two functions not be overloaded by return type alone?", "a": "The compiler selects the overloaded function from the arguments at the call. If both functions take the same arguments, the call does not show which function is wanted, even if the return types differ. The program is rejected."},
        {"q": "What does a static local variable do?", "a": "A static local variable is created once and keeps its value after the function returns. The next call sees the value left by the previous call. An ordinary local variable is created again on each call."},
